    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Westmoreland received its name in 1752 when Governor Benning Wentworth regranted the area, honoring John Fane, 7th Earl of Westmorland.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Westmoreland%2C_New_Hampshire",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The 1762 Park Hill Meetinghouse, famed for its Paul Revere bell, was moved by oxen in 1779 and renovated in 1826.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26,
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Westmoreland%2C_New_Hampshire",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Westmoreland borders the Connecticut River to the west, while Hyland Hill—its 1,510‑foot summit—offers a popular hiking spot on private land.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28,
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Westmoreland%2C_New_Hampshire",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Off Poocham Road, Pow‑Wow Rock—a 12‑by‑14‑by‑8‑foot glacial boulder—served as a gathering place for indigenous pow‑wows and prisoner exchanges.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20,
        21,
        22,
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Westmoreland%2C_New_Hampshire",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Westmoreland has long been an agricultural town, its rolling hills dotted with arable farmland that supports local farms to this day.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Westmoreland%2C_New_Hampshire",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "State Routes 12 and 63 crisscross Westmoreland, linking its rural communities to neighboring towns and the Connecticut River corridor.",
